  "summary": "Eight November 2026 preprints target optimizers, shot budgets, noise calibration and input encoding for variational and reservoir-style quantum learning models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the training pipeline of parameterized quantum circuits rather than new model classes. On optimization, a Krotov-inspired discrete-adjoint optimizer uses a hybrid online-to-batch schedule, and frozen LLMs proposing circuit parameters are effective on structured Hamiltonians but underperform Adam on general random ones. On cost and noise, a learned controller prunes qubit-wise commuting measurement groups in VQE gradients, reporting 49.6% shot savings. Reusable calibration mitigates hardware noise to restore QNN trainability. A parallel thread uses fixed quantum dynamics with trained linear readouts. Data re-uploading in physical reservoirs improves higher-order nonlinear processing and chaotic time-series tasks. Multi-layer quantum extreme learning machines fitted via MMD match a classical random-feature baseline. Benchmarks of variational quantum RNNs quantify memory and nonlinear capacity against classical baselines. Architecture search with adaptive non-local observables is ablated for quantum RL. Several items report parity with classical baselines rather than advantage.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
